WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.460
You cut both copies of a gene in every cell of a dish.

2
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.140
Why is half the dish still making the protein?

3
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:14.880
Look at one cell. It comes from a diploid line, so it carries two copies of the

4
00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.680
gene. We aim Cas9 at both copies.

5
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:19.960
The cut is the easy part.

6
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.880
What the cell does next is the real question.

7
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:25.613
Cas9 is aimed by a guide.

8
00:00:26.553 --> 00:00:29.813
The target-binding part of the guide is 20 nucleotides long.

9
00:00:30.653 --> 00:00:33.553
It must sit right next to a short motif called the PAM.

10
00:00:34.513 --> 00:00:36.453
The preferred PAM reads NGG.

11
00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:41.373
Cas9 ignored DNA that matched the guide but had no nearby PAM.

12
00:00:41.373 --> 00:00:46.493
In the original test, the cut fell three base pairs upstream of the PAM.

13
00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:49.553
The cut is where your control ends.

14
00:00:50.373 --> 00:00:52.113
The cell repairs the break on its own.

15
00:00:52.973 --> 00:00:54.053
One route is end joining.

16
00:00:54.973 --> 00:00:56.033
It needs no template.

17
00:00:56.833 --> 00:00:58.793
It can leave insertions and deletions.

18
00:00:59.673 --> 00:01:02.013
Another route is homology-directed repair.

19
00:01:02.853 --> 00:01:04.553
It copies a template that you supply.

20
00:01:05.493 --> 00:01:08.373
So sequencing the pool shows a spread of different alleles.

21
00:01:09.283 --> 00:01:11.003
Now count the alleles in the pool.

22
00:01:11.943 --> 00:01:13.183
Ten percent are unedited.

23
00:01:14.123 --> 00:01:16.123
Thirty-five percent lose one base pair.

24
00:01:16.883 --> 00:01:18.383
Twenty-five percent gain one.

25
00:01:19.163 --> 00:01:23.743
Fifteen percent lose three, five percent lose six, and ten percent lose seven.

26
00:01:24.503 --> 00:01:27.383
A length that is not a multiple of three shifts the frame.

27
00:01:28.283 --> 00:01:30.523
Lose one, gain one and lose seven all shift it.

28
00:01:31.423 --> 00:01:34.583
Add them up. Seventy percent are frameshifted.

29
00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:37.253
Each cell has two alleles.

30
00:01:38.053 --> 00:01:39.593
Assume they are edited independently.

31
00:01:40.613 --> 00:01:43.853
The chance that one allele is frameshifted is 0.7.

32
00:01:44.713 --> 00:01:45.793
For both, multiply.

33
00:01:46.853 --> 00:01:50.493
0.7 times 0.7 is 0.49.

34
00:01:51.433 --> 00:01:54.833
So 49 percent of cells have both alleles frameshifted.

35
00:01:55.673 --> 00:01:59.413
About half the cells still carry at least one in-frame or unedited allele.

36
00:01:59.413 --> 00:02:00.413
The chance that one allele is 0 .7 is 0 .7 times 0 .7 is 0 .7.

37
00:02:00.467 --> 00:02:04.487
So a cut, a mutated allele, and a knockout are three different states.

38
00:02:05.487 --> 00:02:08.587
Only the third is a knockout, a loss of the gene's function.

39
00:02:09.347 --> 00:02:11.687
A pool like this is a mosaic, not a null.

40
00:02:12.647 --> 00:02:17.127
Derive clones, genotype them and check that function is gone before you call the

41
00:02:17.127 --> 00:02:17.987
phenotype a knockout.
